 for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>51\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 57\u00b0F, and the record is 77\u00b0F (1955). The low of <strong>39\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Atlantic City has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Atlantic City's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Atlantic City has set <strong class=\"sig\">55 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 118 that chance alone would give, and <strong>158 record highs</strong>, more than the 118 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (64). Atlantic City's all-time high is 106\u00b0F, on June 28, 1969.","t_chg":"Atlantic City has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 81 complete years: Atlantic City's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.19\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.02 to +0.35), about +1.5\u00b0F since 1944. Atlantic City's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.44\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.33 to +0.56), about +3.6\u00b0F since 1944.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 4 of 12 months. Highs rose in 11 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Atlantic City has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Atlantic City's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+1.00 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.22 to +1.78), about +8.1 inches since 1944. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 45.9 inches a year. The wettest year was 2018 (68.57 in); the driest, 1965 (25.27 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 14 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}